.TH PLAYMOVIE 1
.SH NAME
playmovie \- play a movie, video paced against the audio
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B playmovie
[
.B -s
]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Playmovie
plays a QuickTime or MP4 file.
.IR quicktime (2)
locates the coded samples of both tracks in Limbo;
.IR audio (3)
decodes the audio to PCM and
.IR draw (3)
decodes the video into draw images.
Nothing here knows how AAC or H.264 work, and nothing in the kernel
knows what an MP4 is.
.PP
The audio is the clock, and it has to be.
A sound card consumes samples at its own fixed rate, so a write to
.B /dev/audio
blocks when the device's buffer is full; feeding it paces the whole
loop for free and gives a position to fit the video to.
Driving the other way \(em showing frames on a timer and playing audio
to match \(em means resampling audio whenever the timer drifts, which
is audible.
.PP
The position the audio clock reports is what has been handed to the
device, which runs ahead of what has been heard by however deep its
buffer is.
That offset is constant, so it does not accumulate: it shifts the whole
picture by a fixed amount rather than letting the two tracks drift
apart, which is the thing that matters.
.PP
Frames are held in a small ring of images and shown in presentation
order.
This is not optional.
Coded samples are in
.I decode
order, and a stream with B-frames presents them in a different one, so
several decoded frames have to exist at once before any can be shown at
the right time.
Pacing frames in the order they decode gives visibly wrong timing: on
.B /lib/movies/av.mov
it puts frames up to 317ms from where they belong, against 19ms with
the ring.
If the ring is ever too small for the stream's reordering depth a frame
would have to be shown after a later one; that is checked for and
reported rather than passing silently.
.PP
The
.B -s
option reports the synchronisation error without opening
.BR /dev/audio ,
so the file plays as fast as it decodes.
The measurement is the same either way, which is the point of having
it: the drift is a property of the arithmetic, not of the speed the
loop happens to run at.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/playmovie.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR quicktime (2),
.IR audio (3),
.IR draw (3),
.IR vstreamtest (1),
.IR adectest (1)
.SH BUGS
The worst error is reported and the frames are counted, but no frame is
ever put on the screen: each is decoded into an image and dropped.
What is tested is the timing, not the display.
.PP
There is no seeking, no pause and no way to stop before the end.
.PP
A clip shorter than the audio device's buffer never blocks, so it plays
in less than its own duration and the pacing is not exercised at all.
.B /lib/movies/av.mov
is one such clip: at 1.07s it finishes in 0.9s.
.I Mkmovie ,
alongside the emu source, will build a longer one \(em 80 frames plays
in 8.01s for 8s of audio, with the error still bounded at 22ms.
.PP
One video track and one audio track, the first of each.
Subtitles and multiple audio tracks are ignored.
